<v Daniel>Just gut feelings, mostly.</v>

8
00:00:48.782 --> 00:00:57.587
<v Maya>Right. Gut feelings. People arguing over what it fundamentally means to be smart or, you know, they pointed a chat bot writing a poem and just assume we're like six months away from the singularity.</v>

9
00:00:57.851 --> 00:01:08.997
<v Daniel>Yeah. So our mention for today's deep dive is to slice right through all that hype. We're looking at a brand new, highly disciplined economics paper. It was published on September 14th, 2026.</v>

10
00:01:09.237 --> 00:01:10.078
<v Maya>Hot off the presses.</v>

11
00:01:10.318 --> 00:01:19.743
<v Daniel>Yeah, literally. It's called The Economics of Recursive Self-Improvement by Tom Cunningham and his eight co-authors at METR, the Elasticity Institute.</v>

12
00:01:20.378 --> 00:01:34.762
<v Maya>And the core contribution of this paper, the real paradigm shift here, is that it takes a very messy, abstract, frankly, kind of chaotic argument about our future and it turns it into a measurable mathematical quantity.</v>

13
00:01:34.982 --> 00:01:35.903
<v Daniel>Which is wild.</v>

14
00:01:36.003 --> 00:01:43.125
<v Maya>It is. They basically built a framework to track the intelligence explosion the exact same way a central bank would track inflation or GDP.</v>

15
00:01:43.285 --> 00:01:52.494
<v Daniel>I love that. And we're going to give you their headline answer right up front today, just so you have an anchor for everything we are about to talk about. Are we currently in a runaway sci-fi intelligence explosion?</v>

16
00:01:52.614 --> 00:01:53.695
<v Maya>The math says not yet.</v>

17
00:01:53.896 --> 00:01:58.080
<v Daniel>Not yet. But the number they are tracking is undeniably rising. Okay, let's unpack this.</v>

18
00:01:58.445 --> 00:02:05.431
<v Maya>So to wrap our heads around the math of the future, we kind of have to ground ourselves in the economic realities of the past first.</v>

19
00:02:05.672 --> 00:02:06.733
<v Daniel>Right, the background context.</v>

20
00:02:06.873 --> 00:02:21.806
<v Maya>Exactly. There's this foundational line of thinking from Avion Jones and Jones back in 2017. They modeled how automating the production of ideas could lead to accelerating or even explosive growth.</v>

21
00:02:21.906 --> 00:02:22.727
<v Daniel>Be optimistic, you.</v>

22
00:02:22.878 --> 00:02:34.944
<v Maya>But that optimistic theory sits on top of this massive, honestly somewhat depressing counterweight in economics. There's a famous paper by Bloom and his co-authors called Our Ideas Getting Harder to Find.</v>

23
00:02:35.124 --> 00:02:36.364
<v Daniel>And the answer is yes, they are.</v>

24
00:02:36.444 --> 00:02:48.950
<v Maya>Yeah, very much yes. They documented this reality that in traditional industries, I mean, whether you're looking at crop yields in agriculture or, you know, squeezing more transistors onto a semiconductor research, productivity is falling.</v>

25
00:02:48.950 --> 00:02:51.072
<v Daniel>It's like we're running on a treadmill that just keeps speeding up.</v>

26
00:02:51.192 --> 00:03:00.842
<v Maya>Exactly. We have to throw an ever increasing number of human researchers and billions more dollars at a problem just to maintain a flat, constant rate of growth.</v>

27
00:03:00.862 --> 00:03:02.524
<v Daniel>Because the low hanging fruit is already gone.</v>

28
00:03:02.904 --> 00:03:11.293
<v Maya>Right. To keep Moore's Law alive, the semiconductor industry has had to massively increase its R&D effort decade after decade.</v>

29
00:03:11.656 --> 00:03:24.787
<v Daniel>So that brings us to the big question Cunningham and his team at METR are asking in this new 2026 paper. Is AI going to be the very first technology in human history to actually escape that historical trap?</v>

30
00:03:25.087 --> 00:03:29.451
<v Maya>Right. And to answer that, they use this incredibly clean graphical framework.</v>

31
00:03:29.531 --> 00:03:33.895
<v Daniel>Yeah, I love how they laid this out. They basically map out AI development as a directed graph.</v>

32
00:03:34.184 --> 00:03:36.407
<v Maya>So think of it like an audio setup at a concert.</v>

33
00:03:36.607 --> 00:03:37.549
<v Daniel>Oh, that's a good way to put it.</v>

34
00:03:37.589 --> 00:03:49.265
<v Maya>Yeah, you have your microphones, your amps, your speakers. In their graph, the different pieces of equipment are the nodes, the outputs, things like algorithmic efficiency or AI capabilities.</v>

35
00:03:49.345 --> 00:03:50.867
<v Daniel>And the cables connecting everything together.</v>

36
00:03:51.194 --> 00:04:00.100
<v Maya>Those are what economists call elasticities. It's just a measure of responsiveness. So how much does a percentage change in one input affect the output?</v>

37
00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:03.982
<v Daniel>Right. Like if you turn up the mic volume by 10 percent, how much louder does the speaker get?</v>

38
00:04:04.397 --> 00:04:14.527
<v Maya>Exactly. And the math to figure out a feedback loop here is surprisingly simple. A feedback loop strength is just the product of the elasticities along it. You just multiply them.</v>

39
00:04:14.587 --> 00:04:18.331
<v Daniel>And then the total elasticity is what you get when you sum over all those different loops.</v>

40
00:04:18.571 --> 00:04:30.880
<v Maya>Right. Which brings us to the knife edge. This is where the magic numbers come in. If the total elasticity of algorithmic improvement with respect to the stock of existing algorithms is below one.</v>

41
00:04:31.000 --> 00:04:32.521
<v Daniel>Then the growth just fizzles out.</v>

42
00:04:32.601 --> 00:04:42.846
<v Maya>Right. It behaves just like normal industries. It gets harder over time and it slows down. Now, if the number is exactly one, you get exponential growth, but it's balancing right on a knife edge.</v>

43
00:04:42.907 --> 00:04:43.847
<v Daniel>But if it's above one.</v>

44
00:04:44.087 --> 00:04:49.490
<v Maya>If it's above one, that is self-sustaining acceleration. That is your runaway intelligence explosion.</v>

45
00:04:50.211 --> 00:04:52.652
<v Daniel>Okay. So how do they actually model getting to that number?</v>

46
00:04:52.963 --> 00:05:02.229
<v Maya>While the paper walks through a progression of models, they start really simple, going back to Jones in 1995, where it's just human R&D labor producing algorithmic improvements.</v>

47
00:05:02.269 --> 00:05:05.491
<v Daniel>Very straightforward. Humans do work. Algorithms get better.</v>

48
00:05:05.831 --> 00:05:14.357
<v Maya>Right. But then they introduce the baseline, RSI model recursive self-improvement. This adds AI capabilities, which they label as C.</v>

49
00:05:14.717 --> 00:05:21.899
<v Daniel>And this is the core feedback loop. Algorithms improve capabilities, and then those capabilities turn around and improve the algorithms.</v>

50
00:05:22.159 --> 00:05:24.661
<v Maya>Exactly. But here's where it gets really interesting.</v>

51
00:05:24.701 --> 00:05:25.822
<v Daniel>Yeah, this part blew my mind.</v>

52
00:05:25.982 --> 00:05:34.487
<v Maya>The paper makes this vital conceptual move. They divide AI capabilities into two totally different categories, narrow and broad.</v>

53
00:05:34.567 --> 00:05:36.469
<v Daniel>Which makes so much sense when you think about it.</v>

54
00:05:36.569 --> 00:05:47.698
<v Maya>It does. Because AI might get incredibly good at optimizing its own AI R&D benchmarks, like writing code or tweaking neural nets. Those are narrow capabilities.</v>

55
00:05:47.798 --> 00:05:50.560
<v Daniel>And those narrow capabilities feed directly back into the loop.</v>

56
00:05:50.773 --> 00:06:01.660
<v Maya>Right, but then you have broad capabilities. These are the skills needed for economically valuable real-world work, things like managing a supply chain or making a medical diagnosis.</v>

57
00:06:01.700 --> 00:06:05.842
<v Daniel>And in their graph, those broad capabilities just kind of dangle off the side.</v>

58
00:06:06.162 --> 00:06:17.369
<v Maya>They dangle. They are outputs, not inputs. So you could have a situation where an AI is a complete genius at optimizing its own code in a lab, but it doesn't get any better at doing actual valuable work for us.</v>

59
00:06:17.872 --> 00:06:21.354
<v Daniel>A mathematical explosion that nobody in the real economy even notices.</v>

60
00:06:21.494 --> 00:06:32.098
<v Maya>Exactly. And even inside that lab, the loop can easily break. The authors talk about these growth spurts. AI might accelerate one specific sub-algorithm.</v>

61
00:06:32.118 --> 00:06:33.659
<v Daniel>Like it gets really good at data sorting.</v>

62
00:06:33.719 --> 00:06:38.301
<v Maya>Right. Massive growth spurt there. But then it stalls out completely because it hits a bottleneck.</v>

63
00:06:38.441 --> 00:06:40.142
<v Daniel>What kind of bottlenecks are we talking about here?</v>

64
00:06:40.545 --> 00:06:53.893
<v Maya>Things that an AI physically cannot optimize on its own, like human labor for specific evaluations or experimental compute, training compute, or just raw data. Any of those can break the loop.</v>

65
00:06:54.074 --> 00:06:58.616
<v Daniel>Because it doesn't matter how smart the algorithm is if you don't have the literal computer chips to run the experiment.</v>

66
00:06:58.757 --> 00:07:08.783
<v Maya>Exactly. And this brings in some great work from Davidson et al., also from 2026, about economic feedback loops. Because it's not just about algorithms improving algorithms. It's about money.</v>

67
00:07:09.192 --> 00:07:12.514
<v Daniel>Right. AI makes revenue. Revenue buys more compute and data.</v>

68
00:07:12.694 --> 00:07:21.799
<v Maya>Right. And for the acceleration to happen, the condition becomes the dominant eigenvalue of a feedback matrix exceeding one, which is a lot of math jargon.</v>

69
00:07:21.939 --> 00:07:24.540
<v Daniel>But basically means the financial engine has to compound, too.</v>

70
00:07:24.681 --> 00:07:33.946
<v Maya>Yes. If we connect this to the bigger picture, it grounds this whole wild tech theory in very harsh economic reality. You have to pay for the data centers.</v>

71
00:07:34.126 --> 00:07:43.169
